{"cell":{"codeSha256":"a04c0cf99cf54cc421c27f0abda93742add6d87a567e6efc570e5ad8f3a957a3","config":{"column":null,"disabled":false,"expand_output":false,"hide_code":true},"id":"nWHF","name":"variograms_intro"},"console":[],"outcome":"completed","output":{"channel":"output","data":"<div style=\"display: flex;flex: 1;flex-direction: column;justify-content: flex-start;align-items: normal;flex-wrap: nowrap;gap: 0.5rem\"><span class=\"markdown prose dark:prose-invert contents\"><h2 id=\"2-the-variograms-per-domain\">2 · The variograms per domain</h2>\n<span class=\"paragraph\">Each row is one domain: its model and sliders, its vertical variogram along the CPTs and its horizontal\none between CPTs at one level. The dots are the experimental variogram, as large as their number of\npairs of samples, and faint below the number a lag needs to count in the fit. The line is the model of\nthe sliders. Exponential, spherical and Gaussian models are each fitted by weighted least squares with a\nsill of 1, and a domain starts with the one that misfits least, unless the saved variograms are of this\nparameter and trend. Choosing another model for a domain starts its sliders from that model's fit. Both\ndirections share the nugget. Each chosen parameter has its own models and sliders.</span></span><div style=\"display: flex;flex: 1;flex-direction: row;justify-content: space-between;align-items: normal;flex-wrap: nowrap;gap: 0.5rem\"><marimo-ui-element object-id=\"nWHF-projection-e2cbddde8a7519930eb7f11acb851b792f8d08d7e9c5603f84ba4ebf901a07e2-ui-nWHF-root-1-4f53cda18c2baa0c0354bb5f9a3ecbe5ed12ab4d8e11ba873c2f11161202b945\" random-id=\"nWHF-projection-e2cbddde8a7519930eb7f11acb851b792f8d08d7e9c5603f84ba4ebf901a07e2-ui-nWHF-root-1-4f53cda18c2baa0c0354bb5f9a3ecbe5ed12ab4d8e11ba873c2f11161202b945-random\"><marimo-radio data-initial-value=\"&quot;qc (cone resistance)&quot;\" data-label=\"&quot;\\u003cspan class=\\&quot;markdown prose dark:prose-invert contents\\&quot;\\u003e\\u003cspan cla